        "thinking": "So even with the UI built, wrangler is still returning the fallback HTML from our Hono app instead of the static files from `ui/dist`. This means the `pages_build_output_dir` setting in wrangler.toml is NOT being used by wrangler 4 to serve static assets.\n\nLet me check the wrangler.toml more carefully. The `pages_build_output_dir` setting is for Cloudflare Pages, not Workers. For Workers with static assets, you need to use the `[assets]` section.\n\nIn wrangler 4, to serve static assets alongside a Worker, you use:\n\n\nConfiguring the assets section...\n\nI'm realizing that with wrangler 4.26.1, the `[assets]` configuration should handle static file serving, but I need to verify whether this should replace or coexist with `pages_build_output_dir` and understand how Cloudflare Workers Assets actually works in this context. I need to clarify the routing behavior for an SPA with Workers Assets—the key issue is that static files get served first, but SPA routes like `/companies/123` need to fall back to `index.html` rather than hitting the Worker's catch-all, which means I might need to handle this fallback logic within the Worker itself or configure the assets binding to serve `index.html` for non-matching routes. I need to configure the Assets binding in `wrangler.toml` and then use `env.ASSETS.fetch()` in the Worker to serve `index.html` for unmatched routes, since Workers can't read files directly from disk. I could implement the SPA fallback by fetching `index.html` through the `env.ASSETS` binding when a route doesn't match a static file, but there's a simpler approach—Cloudflare's `not_found_handling = \"single-page-application\"` config option automatically serves `index.html` for any 404s, which is the recommended way to handle SPAs.\n\nThe routing order is important here: static assets get priority for exact matches, the Worker handles everything else, and if the Worker returns a 404, the assets layer kicks in with the SPA fallback. Since my API routes are handled by the Worker itself, they won't accidentally fall through to this fallback behavior. I need to clarify how the routing actually works with Workers and static assets. When a Worker script is configured, the request hits the Worker first, and then the Worker can explicitly call `env.ASSETS.fetch()` to serve static files, or handle the request itself—the automatic static asset serving doesn't happen unless the Worker delegates to it.\n\nSo the pattern here is: API routes get handled directly by the Worker, while UI routes should delegate to the ASSETS binding, which will serve matching files or fall back to `index.html` in SPA mode. I need to set up a catch-all route that calls the ASSETS binding, add the Fetcher type to the Env interface, and configure the assets section in wrangler.toml.\n\nConfiguring wrangler.toml...\n\nActually, I should verify how `not_found_handling` behaves when there's both a Worker script and assets—the Worker runs first, so the 404 handling only kicks in when ASSETS itself returns a 404, which is exactly what I need for the SPA fallback.\n\nLet me check the wrangler 4 docs to confirm `not_found_handling` is available and what the options are. Good—it supports `\"none\"` (default 404), `\"single-page-application\"` (serve index.html), and `\"404-page\"` (serve 404.html).\n\nNow I'll implement the fix by updating `wrangler.toml` to remove the Pages-specific config and add the assets binding with SPA mode enabled.\n\nUpdating the TypeScript interfaces and worker code to use the ASSETS fetcher for the SPA fallback, then rebuilding and testing.",
